meta {
  name: Mark Contact Deceased
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/contacts/{{householdMemberContactId}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  Authorization: Bearer {{clientAdminToken}}
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "deceasedAt": "2026-02-14"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("records the death as a calendar date, echoed back exactly", function () {
    // Parsed to UTC midnight from explicit components, like a date of birth —
    // `new Date("2026-02-14")` re-rendered west of Greenwich shows the 13th.
    expect(res.getBody().deceasedAt).to.equal("2026-02-14");
  });

  test("the person is untouched otherwise", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    // A date, not a delete: the contact stays, with its name, on every
    // historical policy, quote, activity and ticket.
    expect(body.firstName).to.equal("Dana");
    expect(body.lastName).to.equal("Tyson");
  });

  test("no successor is demanded of a contact who leads nothing", function () {
    // `Restore Primary Contact` handed the household back to its original
    // primary, so this member now leads no household and there is no succession
    // to settle. The same request one step earlier is a 409.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.not.equal(409);
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /contacts/:id — recording a death

  The success half of PAC-91 §7, on a contact who leads no household.

  - **Module:** `clients`. **Permission:** `clients:write`.
  - Sent as the agency owner, for the reason `Login as Client Manager` explains.

  ## A date, not a boolean and not a delete

  Nothing in the schema modelled this before — `grep -i deceased` over the repo
  returned nothing at all. Deleting the row was never the alternative: the
  person is on historical policies, quotes, activities and tickets, every one of
  which has to keep rendering their name. A boolean would lose the one thing an
  office needs to reconcile a policy against.

  Clearing it (`"deceasedAt": null`, see `Clear Contact Deceased`) undoes it. A
  death recorded in error has to be reversible, which is half the reason this is
  a date rather than a delete.

  ## What changes for a deceased contact

  Everything forward-looking, and nothing historical:

  - **Lead intake** stops matching them. The fuzzy scorer never sees them, and a
    *definite* identity hit (same name, DOB, and phone or email) refuses the
    submission with **409 `contact_deceased`** rather than filing a new lead
    against a dead person.
  - **Not offered as a successor** when a household names a new primary, and
    refused outright if named — `POST /households/:id/primary-contact` answers
    409 `contact_deceased`.
  - **Not offered as a defensive driver** on a new sold deal.
  - **Quote prefill** seeds a living member instead.
  - **Outbound details** stop being offered: the Household page's click-to-call
    and mailto go inert, and the Producer Dashboard's Call / Text / Email quick
    actions grey out with a reason.

  They stay on the household roster, on the Lead Detail card (with a banner
  saying not to make contact), and on every record they were already on.

  ⚠ They also stay in the **identity indexes**. A dead person is still a person
  whose row must not be duplicated, and a duplicate check that disagreed with
  its own index would turn a duplicate into an unexplained E11000 at the write.
  That is why intake refuses rather than silently creating a second contact.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | A future date, a malformed date, or `successorContactId` / `allowNoPrimary` sent without `deceasedAt` |
  | 404 | Unknown contact, malformed id, or one the caller's scope cannot reach |
  | 409 `primary_contact_succession_required` | The contact leads a household and no successor was named — see the previous request |
}
